=> MongoDB Basics

    -> What is a Collection?

        -> A Collection is like a table in a relational database.
        -> It stores multiple documents.
        -> Example:
        
           Collection: users

           {
               name: "Vinay",
               age: 27
           }

           {
               name: "Rahul",
               age: 30
           }

    -> What is a Document?

        -> A Document is a single record stored inside a collection.
        -> It is stored in BSON (Binary JSON) format.
        -> Example:

           {
               _id: ObjectId(...),
               name: "Vinay",
               age: 27,
               email: "vinay@gmail.com"
           }

    -> What is a Query?

        -> A Query is a request to MongoDB to retrieve, insert, update or delete documents.
        -> Example:

           db.users.find({age: {$gt: 25}})

    -> What is a Cursor? ⭐ (Very Important)

        -> Cursor is an object returned by the find() method.
        -> It points to the query result.
        -> Cursor does NOT store all documents in memory at once.
        -> It fetches documents lazily (one by one or in batches) as needed.
        -> This makes MongoDB memory efficient.

        Flow:

                Collection
                     │
               db.users.find()
                     │
                     ▼
                  Cursor
                     │
          ┌──────────┴──────────┐
          ▼                     ▼
      Document 1           Document 2 ...

    -> Why does MongoDB use Cursor?

        -> Suppose a collection contains 10 million documents.
        -> Loading all documents into memory would consume huge memory.
        -> Instead, MongoDB returns a Cursor.
        -> Cursor retrieves documents only when required.

    -> Cursor Methods

        -> sort()
        -> limit()
        -> skip()
        -> next()
        -> hasNext()
        -> forEach()
        -> toArray()

        Example:

        db.users.find()
                .sort({age:1})
                .limit(5)

        Flow:

            Collection
                 │
                 ▼
              find()
                 │
                 ▼
              Cursor
                 │
                 ▼
            sort(age)
                 │
                 ▼
             limit(5)
                 │
                 ▼
          Final Documents

    -> Collection Methods

        -> find()
        -> findOne()
        -> insertOne()
        -> insertMany()
        -> updateOne()
        -> updateMany()
        -> replaceOne()
        -> deleteOne()
        -> deleteMany()
        -> countDocuments()
        -> estimatedDocumentCount()

    -> Why countDocuments() cannot be used after find()?

        Wrong:

        db.users.find({age: {$gt:30}})
                .countDocuments()

        Reason:

            Collection
                 │
                 ▼
              find()
                 │
                 ▼
              Cursor
                 │
          countDocuments() ❌

        -> countDocuments() is NOT a Cursor method.
        -> It is a Collection method.

        Correct:

        db.users.countDocuments({
            age: {$gt:30}
        })

        Flow:

            Collection
                 │
      countDocuments(filter)
                 │
                 ▼
            Returns Count

    -> Difference Between Collection and Cursor

        Collection

            -> Represents the actual collection in MongoDB.
            -> Can perform CRUD operations.
            -> Returns documents or modifies data.

        Cursor

            -> Represents the result of a query.
            -> Used to iterate over query results.
            -> Supports operations like sorting, limiting and skipping.

    -> Easy Way to Remember

        Collection
            -> Owns the data.

        find()
            -> Creates a Cursor.

        Cursor
            -> Navigates through the matching documents.

        countDocuments()
            -> Counts matching documents directly from the Collection.

